  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 AM CDT Mon Jun 15 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a zonal flow aloft prevailing across the Western\nHigh Plains. Near the surface, weak high pressure is drifting east\nthrough western Kansas.\n\nThunderstorm chances (30-50%) return to portions of southwest Kansas \nlate today as the SREF indicates a series of H5 vort maxima cycling \nsoutheast off a ridge axis situated near the Pacific Coast, and\nthrough the Colorado Rockies into the Western High Plains. Moisture\nreturn on the backside of a departing surface high will push dewpoints\nwell up into the 50s(F) in extreme southwest Kansas, introducing\nan instability axis near the Colorado line ahead of a developing\nsurface low with MUCAPE values pushing upward of 1000 to potentially\n1500 J/kg. Despite a less than robust flow aloft, steepening \nlow/mid-level lapse rates combined with sufficient instability will\nlead to likely thunderstorm development across extreme southeast\nColorado late afternoon, then spreading into southwest Kansas this\nevening. As suggested by the HREF, the best chance for any appreciable\nrainfall will generally be southwest of a Scott City to Medicine Lodge\nline where there is a 20-40% probability of 6-hr QPF exceeding 0.1\nof an inch by late evening. Diminishing rain chances shift eastward\nTuesday under a weakening northwest flow aloft and weak forcing in\nthe lower levels.\n\nWarmer temperatures are likely today as southerlies return behind\nthe aforementioned departing surface high while surface low pressure\ndevelops in southeast Colorado. This will pull warmer air into the\narea with H85 temperatures a little above 15C in central Kansas to\nnear 20C in extreme southwest Kansas. With the HREF painting a 70-90%\nprobability of temperatures topping 80F, look for widespread afternoon\nhighs in the 80s(F). The warming trend continues Tuesday with the\nhelp of prevailing southerlies, pushing afternoon highs up into the\nmid 80s(F) to near 90F.",
